TASK: Generate a Workflow Definition Document (WDD) (Schema: wrd-7.0)
You are analyzing a scientific workflow codebase to produce a Workflow Definition Document (WDD) as defined in `wdd_design-v7.md` and `template_separate_docs/template_WDD.yml`.

PURPOSE
The WDD captures workflow logical structure only (what the workflow is), independent of deployment/hardware/runtime behavior.

HARD SCOPE RULES
- Use static code analysis only.
- Do not include deployment details (node counts, storage tiers, placement policy, hardware constraints, runtime bandwidth, performance targets, resolved cardinality counts for input-determined data objects).
- If a value cannot be determined, use "unknown" and explain why in the relevant field.
- Mark assumptions inline with [ASSUMPTION] and also add a corresponding `translation_metadata.field_confidence_records` entry.

OUTPUT
Produce exactly one YAML file: `wdd.yaml`.

REQUIRED TOP-LEVEL SECTIONS (all required)
1. metadata
2. stages
3. tasks
4. data_objects
5. pc_edges
6. workflow_graph
7. workflow_patterns
8. io_behavioral_hints
9. execution_profiles
10. translation_metadata

REQUIRED ID CONVENTIONS
- stage_id:  stage:<descriptor>
- task_id:   task:<descriptor>
- data_id:   data:<descriptor>
- edge_id:   pc:<producer_task>-><consumer_task>:<pattern>
- hint_id:   hint:<short_name>

SCHEMA-CRITICAL ENUMS (must match exactly)
- pc_edges[].pc_pattern:                1_to_1 | 1_to_n | n_to_1 | n_to_n
- pc_edges[].coupling:                  tight | loose
- pc_edges[].communication_pattern:     shared_file | file_per_producer | in_memory | streaming_channel
- pc_edges[].data_volume_class:         small | medium | large
- workflow_patterns.primary_pattern:    cascading | iterative | scatter_gather | pipeline | broadcast | checkpointing | conditional | hybrid
- tasks[].parallelism_model.type:       serial | embarrassingly_parallel | data_parallel | task_parallel
- tasks[].io_dominance:                 compute_bound | io_bound | balanced | unknown
- tasks[].io_access_type:               posix_independent | mpi_io_collective | mpi_io_independent | hdf5_parallel | netcdf4_parallel | python_serial | unknown
- tasks[].restart_model:                idempotent | checkpoint_restart | non_restartable | unknown
- tasks[].output_class:                 intermediate | checkpoint
- data_objects[].category:              input | intermediate | output | checkpoint | config
- data_objects[].persistence:           transient | stage_scoped | workflow_scoped | persistent
- data_objects[].cardinality_source.type: fixed | config_determined | runtime_determined
- io_behavioral_hints[].potential_concern: partial_access | format_mismatch | small_file_overhead | unnecessary_serialization | data_reuse | checkpoint_waste | metadata_overhead | mpi_io_pattern_risk | unknown

REQUIRED METADATA FIELDS
metadata must include: workflow_lineage_id, version, version_notes, deprecated, workflow_name, workflow_description, source_repository, source_commit, source_format, source_file, schema_version (must be "wrd-7.0"), generated_by, generated_at, last_modified_at.

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS FROM DESIGN SPEC
- pc_edges[].data_objects is a list; one edge carries all datasets shared between a producer-consumer pair. Do not create separate edges per dataset for the same task relationship.
- tasks[].io_access_type: infer from imports and API calls. mpi4py + MPI.File.Open + Read_all/Write_all → mpi_io_collective. mpi4py + Read/Write (no _all) → mpi_io_independent. h5py with driver='mpio' → hdf5_parallel. netCDF4 with parallel=True → netcdf4_parallel. Standard Python I/O without MPI → python_serial. Direct POSIX syscalls → posix_independent.
- tasks[].restart_model: infer from checkpoint-read logic at startup, --resume/--restart CLI flags, or "skip if output exists" guards.
- tasks[].restart_checkpoint_dataset: set to the data_id of the checkpoint read at startup when restart_model = checkpoint_restart; null otherwise.
- tasks[].hardware_path: document has_conditional_execution, execution_alternatives (when true), and minimum_resource_assertions (assert statements, argparse checks, MPI size guards visible in source).
- data_objects[].cardinality_source: always populate type, controlling_parameter, config_file, and possible_values.
- workflow_graph is derived from pc_edges and tasks. Must include: integrity_hash (SHA-256 of canonical pc_edges serialization), last_generated, dag_edges, loop_groups, critical_path_hint, control_flow (has_iteration, iteration_description, has_conditional_branches, conditional_description), stage_execution_order, cross_reference (task_to_stage, data_to_producer, data_to_consumers).
- workflow_patterns must include primary_pattern, secondary_patterns, classification_rationale, and pattern_table_row.
- execution_profiles[].required_task_set must be auto-derived by backward DAG traversal from terminal_task_id — never manually specified.
- execution_profiles[].conditional_inclusions: list tasks in this profile's DAG reachability that are only active under a specific EDD parameter condition (e.g., "param:n_chromosomes > 1"). Empty list if no conditional tasks.

REQUIRED HUMAN-ASK FIELDS (required_static_ask)
If not reliably inferable from code, ask and record answers for:
- metadata.workflow_description
- tasks[].description
- tasks[].output_class
- data_objects[].description
- execution_profiles[].profile_name
- execution_profiles[].description

MINIMUM CONFIDENCE TRACKING REQUIREMENTS
translation_metadata.field_confidence_records must include at least:
- all description fields (tasks, data_objects, stages)
- all pc_pattern fields
- workflow_patterns classification fields
- each task: io_dominance, io_access_type, restart_model, behavioral_notes
- all io_behavioral_hints
- all execution_profile proposals

GENERATION PROCEDURE

Step 1 — Survey codebase structure
Read top-level files, README, and orchestration files (Makefile, shell scripts, Nextflow, Pegasus DAX, Parsl, Snakemake, Slurm, Swift/T). Identify the entry point, task definition mechanism, and key config files.

Step 2 — Enumerate stages and tasks
Identify all distinct executable units and their stage membership. Record per-task inputs/outputs and executable names.

Step 3 — Classify io_access_type per task
Inspect import statements and API calls for each task.
- mpi_io_collective:   mpi4py + MPI.File.Open + Read_all / Write_all.
- mpi_io_independent:  mpi4py + MPI.File.Open + Read / Write (not _all). Flag hint:mpi_io_pattern_risk if many ranks share a file.
- hdf5_parallel:       h5py with driver='mpio' (requires parallel HDF5 build).
- netcdf4_parallel:    netCDF4 with parallel=True.
- python_serial:       standard Python I/O (open(), numpy, pandas) without MPI.
- posix_independent:   direct POSIX file I/O without an MPI or Python layer.

Step 4 — Classify restart_model per task
Look for checkpoint-read logic at startup (e.g., if os.path.exists(checkpoint): load(checkpoint)). Look for --resume / --restart / --continue CLI flags. Look for "skip if output exists" guards (if os.path.exists(output): sys.exit(0) → idempotent). Look for tasks that write partial results in-place without atomicity → non_restartable. Set restart_checkpoint_dataset to the checkpoint data_id when restart_model = checkpoint_restart.

Step 5 — Build data object registry
Deduplicate all workflow data objects. Classify category and persistence. For HDF5/netCDF4, extract dataset names, dtypes, and shapes where visible. Populate cardinality_source (type, controlling_parameter, config_file, possible_values). Derive lifecycle fields (creation_phase, last_consumer_phase, retention_window, access_frequency_class, consumer_count) from DAG topology.

Step 6 — Build producer-consumer edges
Map producers and consumers per data object. Classify pc_pattern, coupling, communication_pattern, and data_volume_class with rationale. Capture multi-dataset edges correctly with the data_objects list.

Step 7 — Build workflow graph and pattern classification
Derive workflow_graph including the integrity hash and cross-reference tables. Classify primary and secondary workflow patterns with rationale. Fill the pattern summary row.

Step 8 — Extract I/O behavioral hints
Re-read task source code looking for: partial_access, format_mismatch, small_file_overhead, unnecessary_serialization, data_reuse, checkpoint_waste, metadata_overhead, mpi_io_pattern_risk. For mpi_io_pattern_risk: flag when independent MPI-IO is used with many ranks sharing a file or using strided access where collective I/O would reduce metadata overhead. Include affected tasks and data objects for each hint.

Step 9 — Build execution profiles
Identify all tasks with output_class = checkpoint. Propose one profile per checkpoint task in topological order plus a full profile. Present to the scientist for confirmation. Ensure required_task_set is DAG-derived from terminal_task_id (never manually specified). Populate conditional_inclusions for tasks conditionally active based on EDD parameters. Populate output_dataset_ids.

Step 10 — Assemble translation metadata
Fill source translation metadata, unmapped fields, round-trip gaps, quality warnings, and confidence records.

Step 11 — Validate before finalizing
- Completeness: every file read/written is represented in data_objects.
- Consistency: all references resolve across tasks, data, edges, and stages.
- Pattern accuracy: P-C cardinality matches pc_pattern.
- No deployment leakage.
- Cross-reference integrity matches body content.
- Stage ordering matches DAG.
- workflow_graph.integrity_hash is current against pc_edges.
- Every assumption has [ASSUMPTION] and a confidence record.
- Multi-dataset edges use pc_edges[].data_objects list.
- Every task has a non-null io_access_type (use unknown only with confidence record).
- Every task has a non-null restart_model.

REFERENCE: PATTERN TAXONOMY

P-C Patterns (per-edge)
- 1_to_1   One producer, one consumer. Example: simulation → post-processing.
- 1_to_n   One producer shared across multiple consumers. Example: static input → many analysis tasks; scatter fan-out.
- n_to_1   Several producers → one consumer. Example: aggregation of parallel outputs.
- n_to_n   Multiple producers and consumers with shared I/O. Example: parallel ML training, ensemble workflows.

Workflow-Level Patterns
- cascading      Linear chain of stages. Any P-C structure.
- iterative      Repeats computation with prior output as input. Often 1_to_1.
- scatter_gather Fan-out to parallel tasks, fan-in to aggregator. 1_to_n scatter; n_to_1 gather.
- pipeline       Stages overlap in time with streaming data. 1_to_1 or 1_to_n with loose coupling.
- broadcast      One task provides shared input to many. 1_to_n.
- checkpointing  Saves task state for restart/recovery. Usually 1_to_1 or n_to_1.
- conditional    Tasks execute only if a runtime condition is met. Usually 1_to_1 or 1_to_n.
- hybrid         Combination of the above. Mixed.

io_access_type Values
- mpi_io_collective   mpi4py + MPI.File.Write_all / Read_all.
- mpi_io_independent  mpi4py + MPI.File.Write / Read (no _all).
- hdf5_parallel       h5py with driver='mpio' (parallel HDF5).
- netcdf4_parallel    netCDF4 with parallel=True.
- python_serial       Python file I/O (open, numpy, pandas) without MPI.
- posix_independent   Direct POSIX I/O without Python or MPI layer.
- unknown             Cannot determine from source; add confidence record with requires_human_review: true.

restart_model Values
- idempotent          "Skip if output exists" guard; safe to re-run from scratch.
- checkpoint_restart  Reads checkpoint file at startup (--resume flag or explicit load logic).
- non_restartable     Writes partial results in-place; no recovery mechanism.
- unknown             Cannot determine from code alone; add confidence record with requires_human_review: true.
